00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
 
hurricane laura  made landfall as a category 4 storm  ravaging through the southern states of  the us  now thousands of homes in texas and  louisiana  are submerged in water and many others  are severely damaged hundreds of  thousands of people were ordered  to evacuate but not everyone did  i stayed we have several pets and  everything and i stayed with  the cats and dogs it was wild  around one or so the wind picked up and  i could hear the the neighbor's roof  blew off the side of the house and just  blew this  blew into my house president trump says  he's planning to visit the affected gulf  coast  though not immediately i was going to  texas i was going to lose  louisiana maybe arkansas looks like  arkansas just spoke to the governor it's  going to be in pretty good shape but  they'll need some help but now it turned  out  we got a little bit lucky it was very  big it was very powerful but it passed  quickly  and so everything's on schedule we'll  probably be going on saturday or sunday  mr president i i just would encourage um  uh people to keep uh keep the people of  louisiana  and texas and now arkansas where you've  signed an emergency declaration as the  tropical storm  moves north keep them in your prayers  but also keep in mind  organizations like red cross that are  also on the ground that are providing  assistance and support  to all of the efforts for families  forecasters have warned of a continued  risk to lives as the storm moves inland  deeper into louisiana though it has now  considerably weakened  a chemical fire in the lake charles area  of louisiana has broken out  emergency crews are tackling it and the  governor has warned residents in the  area to shelter  the federal emergency management agency  says resources are in place to help  restore  services to affected areas in the weeks  and months ahead  matthew capucci meteorologist for the  washington post in port arthur texas  joins us now matthew thank you for being  with us hurricane laura was predicted to  hit somewhere between houston and new  orleans and bring with it  unsurvivable storm surges just how bad  was it  you know we had some good news and some  bad news with the storm it looks like  the storm surge may not have been quite  as bad  as originally anticipated some areas did  report about a three to four in  a meter increase in water level  associated with the surge so that is  pretty bad news but the good aspect of  this  was that that wasn't quite as bad as the  six to seven meter storm surge we were  anticipating at the same time  winds gusted upwards of 210 kilometers  per hour in spots  that caused widespread devastation and  that penetrated farther inland than  originally anticipated the windstorm  aspect of it  was much worse than we thought it would  be in fact like a 16 kilometer wide path  of total devastation from this eyewall  that came ashore  resulted in significant damage across  much of the area and  we saw some really significant  structural issues as well  and how does this compare to other  hurricanes you've seen in the past  you know to be honest this is one of the  most memorable wind storm events that  i've seen the entire eyewall was like a  60 kilometer  wide tornado with winds gusting upwards  of 210 kilometers per hour i was driving  down  interstate 10 westbound and it was just  free after tree after tree down in fact  it was pretty much  impassable unless you went to the far  left lane and tractor trailers were  toppled on their size  along the interstate and one thing that  really struck me was the magnitude of  the wind damage  in very narrow swaths i thought i saw  mobile homes  that were completely obliterated  demolished by the heavy winds  i saw gas station canopies that were  lofted and thrown  vehicles that were perhaps moved or  tipped over in the form of those 18  wheelers in the higher profile vehicles  and in addition extensive roof damage as  well  and one of the things that struck me the  most there was a steel truss  building an outbuilding of some sort  that looked to be an industrial complex  that was completely leveled collapsed by  the winds and these rims at this point  were only about 160 to 170 kilometers  per hour  so the fact that we're able to  accomplish these feats really tells you  a lot about how  long the wind stuck around up to three  hours at times and just how widespread  they were  but the hurricane has now been  downgraded in in category does that  mean the worst is over  [Music]  for the most part yes the good news is  that because it's far enough inland we  don't have to worry about any coastal  surge  anymore and i always tell folks run from  the water hide from the wind  the water right now not a big threat  because it's so far  inland but the wind still impressed in  many areas  gusts to around 90 or 100 kilometers per  hour as far north as the architects or  even a tennessee valley and there are  some weather models  that indicate the propensity for this  storm to actually track with  a little bit of a wind signature all the  way to the east coast  so gusty winds likely intensity in ohio  valleys in the coming days  and then the chance this storm can  actually become reinvigorated off the  east coast  and form into a tropical storm once  again sometime in the saturday into  early sunday time frame  now so far in this hurricane season  there have been three  already been three ferocious hurricanes  why is the current uh  season so active i mean this started in  june  exactly you know on average we typically  see us reach the l or the m  names and we've already reached the m  names but but we're talking for an  entire season so we've seen a season's  worth of hurricanes  so far in just 2020. we're about 200  percent  and more active than normal in terms of  the amount of energy output by the  oceans  and one thing that concerns me even  though we just had these two storms  rolled through  the atlantic is still well above average  in terms of water temperatures  with many areas around 32 or 33 degrees  and that is just a powder keg in terms  of the atmosphere  to get another thing i'm seeing right  now a convectively coupled kelvin wave  basically a big area of rising motion  over the atlantic  and that will favor an increased  development chains of storms in the  coming weeks and it looks to pedal back  mid to late september early october  but then in the middle to later portions  of october  likely to rage again so several  additional peaks  within this hurricane season of enhanced  activity and increased likelihood of  storms  but it looks like this season is just  right for development and that does not  look to slow down anytime  soon okay comprehensive insight there  matthew capucci  thank you thank you  you

